A recipe for organizing photos starts with \cmd|jq|, but your
shell says it cannot find the command. Another web page offers
an installer, and a coworker says you already have it. Both
assertions could be true on different machines or in different
shells. Find out what you have before adding software. This
chapter does not require installing anything or contacting
a repository.

\begin{objectives}
\item Distinguish shell discovery, installed-package inventory, and repository search.
\item Identify the supported package channel for the system you are using.
\item Understand what to review before an install, upgrade, or removal.
\end{objectives}

\section{Look for the command where you intend to run it}
\label{sec:35-discover}
\index{command@\texttt{command}}
\index{command lookup}
Use an ordinary, unprivileged \cmd|sh| for the literal examples.
Start with the tool name, not a guess about its package name:

\begin{terminal}
command -v jq
printf 'lookup status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|command -v| asks what the current shell would use for a
command name, without running it. If found, it prints an
identification: typically a pathname for an external
command, but it can report an alias or builtin, too.
Read it before trusting it. A zero status is a successful
lookup, not a guarantee that the program will run. Silence
with a nonzero status usually means the name did not resolve.
Do not replace the recipe's optional tool with an unrelated
command of the same name.

\begin{terminal}
command -v sh
printf '%s\n' "$PATH"
\end{terminal}

The first line provides a comparison lookup for a familiar
shell name. The second shows the search path (Chapter~\ref{ch:28}):
for ordinary \cmd|sh| usage, names of directories separated by
colons. Order matters. A new install can be outside that path
or a different copy can come earlier. The printed path might
contain personal directory names, so inspect it locally rather
than posting it. A session started by a graphical app and one
started in a terminal might have different settings. Run the
lookup in the context that actually failed, rather than assuming
that a successful lookup somewhere else settles it.
Before taking an installation step, compare the three
separate questions in Figure~\ref{fig:35-questions}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-35/questions.tex}
\caption{Three different sources answer three different questions
about a tool.}
\label{fig:35-questions}
\VisualDescription{Three rows pair shell with lookup, local
database with installed inventory, and catalog with availability.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.08in}YY}
\toprule
Source & Can tell you & Cannot by itself tell you\\
\midrule
Current shell & What this name resolves to here. &
Whether the chosen file is managed or up to date.\\
Local inventory & What this package manager records as installed. &
Whether the command can be invoked in this shell.\\
Catalog or cache & What a configured source lists or listed. &
Whether it is installed, runnable, or the latest upstream release.\\
Local manual & Syntax documented by installed manual pages. &
Whether the manual and selected executable came from the same installation.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{terminal}
man 1 jq
\end{terminal}

The \cmd|1| asks for the user-command manual section. If a
page opens, read the name and synopsis, then quit the pager
with \Key{q} if the pager supports it. If no page is found,
the executable can still exist; some packages separate
documentation or install it outside the manual search path.
Conversely, a page can remain after a program is moved. If you
have identified and trust the installed program, the upstream
\cmd|jq| manual documents this check:

\begin{terminal}
jq --version
\end{terminal}

Run that line only if the lookup found a trusted copy, and
compare the version against the feature your recipe needs.
\cmd|--version| is a documented \cmd|jq| option here, not a
portable option promised by POSIX or by every command. Often
the simplest answer is to do the one-off task with a tool
you already know; use optional software when it adds enough
value to justify taking care of it.

\section{Find out which support channel applies}
\label{sec:35-inventory}
\index{package manager}
\index{package repository}
First ask which operating system is running:

\begin{terminal}
uname -s
\end{terminal}

This prints the system name, often \texttt{Linux},
\texttt{Darwin}, \texttt{FreeBSD}, \texttt{OpenBSD}, or
\texttt{NetBSD}. Darwin commonly indicates macOS in this
book's context. Linux is only the kernel part of the answer:
distributions select the rest of the software, including
package managers. When \cmd|uname -s| says Linux, the file
\cmd|/etc/os-release| can identify the distribution if
present. Read it as text, without executing it.

\begin{terminal}
if [ -r /etc/os-release ]; then
    grep -E '^(NAME|VERSION_ID)=' /etc/os-release
fi
\end{terminal}

\cmd|[ -r ... ]| tests readability before \cmd|grep -E| looks
for lines whose keys are \texttt{NAME} or \texttt{VERSION\_ID};
the caret anchors the match at the start and the vertical bar
separates alternatives within the parentheses. A missing
field is possible. Ask your provider or consult the
distribution's own documentation rather than choosing a
different distribution by resemblance.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.12in}YY}
\toprule
System & Typical optional software channel & Local inspection if already available\\
\midrule
Debian or Ubuntu & APT and distribution repositories. &
\texttt{apt-cache policy jq}\\
Standard Fedora editions & DNF and Fedora repositories; RPM records installs. &
\texttt{rpm -q jq}\\
Arch Linux & Pacman and the distribution's package repositories. &
\texttt{pacman -Q jq}\\
macOS & Homebrew is one independent, optional choice. &
\texttt{brew list --versions jq}\\
FreeBSD & \texttt{pkg} and its package repositories. &
\texttt{pkg info jq}, only if the real manager is installed.\\
OpenBSD & Packages built for the corresponding release or snapshot. &
\texttt{pkg\_info}\\
NetBSD & pkgsrc packages built for the applicable system and architecture. &
\texttt{pkg\_info}\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

Use only the example for the manager you have identified.
Debian or Ubuntu readers can compare the view from the local
APT cache with the shell lookup:

\begin{terminal}
apt-cache policy jq
\end{terminal}

The operand is a package name here. \cmd|policy| reports known
versions, priorities and candidates without installing. If
available, the ``Installed'' line describes the local record;
``Candidate'' describes the version the cache and policy
would select. A cache can be empty or out of date. Do not
refresh the cache for this exercise; a refresh contacts
repositories and changes package metadata. This observation
also cannot tell which program your shell will use after
you install anything.

\begin{terminal}
rpm -q jq
\end{terminal}

For a Fedora system, \cmd|rpm -q| queries its local installed
package database, not a server. Expect an installed package
identification or a not-installed message, depending on
the machine. Fedora uses DNF for operations that need
repository selection and dependency planning on standard
Fedora editions. Image-based variants such as the Fedora
Atomic Desktops use different maintenance workflows; check
the edition's help. Do not use RPM to bypass the package
manager's dependency decisions.

\begin{terminal}
pacman -Q jq
\end{terminal}

On Arch Linux and compatible systems using Pacman, the capital
\cmd|-Q| asks the installed-package database. It prints the
package name and version if recorded locally. This is not
the command for synchronizing with package repositories.
Do not confuse it with the lower-case \cmd|-q| option,
which has a different purpose in Pacman.

\begin{terminal}
brew list --versions jq
\end{terminal}

On macOS this is relevant only if Homebrew is already installed
and you are allowed to use it. It lists the installed formula
version where present, not whether your shell has the expected
Homebrew prefix on \cmd|PATH|. Homebrew software is distinct
from Apple-provided tools and system updates. It is fine to
leave an unmanaged laptop as it is if you can complete the task
without the extra tool.

\begin{terminal}
pkg -N
\end{terminal}

On FreeBSD, the base \cmd|pkg| can be a bootstrap utility
instead of the full manager. \cmd|-N| checks for the installed
manager without bootstrapping it. If this check says it is
installed, use \cmd|pkg info jq| for the local record. If not,
stop rather than running a normal package operation: that can
offer to download and install the manager itself.

\begin{terminal}
pkg_info
\end{terminal}

On OpenBSD, this lists packages installed locally. On NetBSD it
can do the same where the pkgsrc tools are available. These
systems use separate package infrastructures even though they
share this command name. An inventory line typically includes
an identifier with a version and sometimes a short
description. Inspect it locally; this no-operand form may
show much more than the one tool you are asking about.

\section{macOS: understand the Homebrew workflow}
\label{sec:35-brew}
\index{Homebrew}
Homebrew is a separate package manager, not a part of macOS
or a replacement for macOS Software Update. A Homebrew
\FirstUse{formula} is a recipe for software usually used
from the command line, such as \cmd|jq|. A
\FirstUse{cask} is commonly used for distributing an app
with its own upstream installer or application bundle.
The distinction matters: a formula can provide a tool on
\cmd|PATH|, but installing a cask for an application does
not imply there will be a same-named terminal command.
Check the app's publisher and any permissions it asks for
as well as the Homebrew entry.

The supported default prefix on Apple silicon is
\texttt{/opt/homebrew}; on Intel macOS it is
\texttt{/usr/local}. A prefix is the root of an installation's
own tree. More than one installation may exist, especially
on a machine that also runs Intel software under Rosetta.
If \cmd|command -v brew| already locates a trusted copy, use
\cmd|brew --prefix| to see which one you are about to use.
Do not guess by architecture or assume results from both
installations belong to one inventory.

\begin{terminal}
command -v brew
brew --prefix
\end{terminal}

Run the second line only if the first identifies the
expected trusted \cmd|brew|. If an installer has completed
but the command is absent, inspect the setup instructions
printed by the official installer. The shell has to know
where to look, which does not depend merely on files
existing under a prefix. For ordinary Homebrew
installs, \cmd|brew shellenv| prints settings tailored
to a shell, including a search path adjustment. Below
is the inspection step for an existing default Apple
silicon installation; on a default Intel installation
the corresponding executable is
\texttt{/usr/local/bin/brew}. Neither line installs Homebrew.

\begin{terminal}
/opt/homebrew/bin/brew shellenv zsh
\end{terminal}

Use the line only for a trusted install at that path,
and do not paste its output from an unknown source. The
argument selects Zsh syntax; the official installer tells
you which initialization command and startup file are
appropriate for your own shell and configuration. It may
recommend evaluating the output of a fully qualified
\cmd|brew shellenv| invocation. Evaluation runs the printed
shell code; its safety depends on which program produced
it. Before editing a startup file, keep a backup or note
the exact line you added. Test a new terminal, keep the
old session for recovery, and check \cmd|command -v brew|
again. If the startup edit was wrong, restore the previous
file or remove only the change you made. If a formula is
``keg-only'', Homebrew is deliberately not linking it into
the usual prefix search location; consult its caveats
rather than forcing links over a system tool.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.19in}YY}
\toprule
Homebrew action & Command forms to recognize & What to check\\
\midrule
Discover & \texttt{brew search jq}; \texttt{brew info jq}. &
Correct name, publisher, formula or cask, and caveats.
These lookups can use network or cached catalog data.\\
Add & \texttt{brew install jq}; \texttt{brew install --cask NAME}. &
Sources, dependencies, available space, and approval. \texttt{NAME}
stands for a verified cask name; do not type it literally.\\
Refresh & \texttt{brew update}. &
Refreshes Homebrew and package definitions, not a request to
upgrade every installed package.\\
Review/upgrade & \texttt{brew outdated}; \texttt{brew upgrade jq}. &
Which installed tools are behind, then what a targeted upgrade
can affect. Some operations may refresh metadata automatically.\\
Remove & \texttt{brew uninstall jq}; \texttt{brew uninstall --cask NAME}. &
Same manager and prefix, data saved, dependencies understood.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

These are reference forms, not a sequence to run; no install,
update, upgrade, removal, or live lookup is part of the
practice. If you decide later that you need Homebrew itself,
start at the official installation documentation. Check your
system meets the currently supported requirements, and
review the install action expected for your machine. On a
managed Mac, get approval before changing tools. Prefer the
expected official tap (a source of Homebrew packages) over
one you found in an old workaround. A package being listed
does not by itself establish that it is the app or command you
want.

If a planned upgrade adds dependencies or removes previous
versions, consider the effect on your work before consenting.
Homebrew may clean up old package versions, so uninstalling
and reinstalling is not necessarily a way back. Back up
relevant configuration and user data, note the installed
version and exact prefix first, and keep a known-good
workflow if the software is important to you. For casks,
avoid opt-in removal of user data when you only meant to
remove an app. If a failure turns out to be a path issue,
repair command selection rather than reinstalling software.
After an authorized change, compare the local inventory
and selected tool, then try a harmless known input.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-35/channels.tex}
\caption{Base-system maintenance and optional software can use
separate supported channels.}
\label{fig:35-channels}
\VisualDescription{System documentation branches to base
maintenance and optional package maintenance.}
\end{figure}

Keep the distinction in Figure~\ref{fig:35-channels} in
mind when following advice on what to update. Someone
reporting a newer optional utility is not necessarily
describing the base version. An organizational support
policy can apply to one or both channels.

\section{Read the plan before accepting it}
\label{sec:35-maintain}
Suppose you need to add a reader for many photo-catalog files.
Start with the documentation for your exact system, release,
and architecture. It tells you where trustworthy packages come
from and how the manager checks repository metadata or package
integrity. Prefer your operating system's supported repository
or your organization's approved channel. A similarly named
package on an unrelated download site is not a substitute.
Do not turn off signature checks, add a repository copied from
a forum, or pipe a downloaded installer into a shell.
On a managed machine, ask whoever maintains it.

A package is an installable bundle plus metadata such as version
and dependencies. A dependency is other software the package
needs. Adding one tool can therefore add several packages, and
an upgrade or removal can affect other tools used on the same
machine. Review the proposed changes in the manager's documented
preview mode if available, or at the official confirmation
step. Check the source, destination, downloads, disk cost, and
any planned removals. Read local documentation before using a
preview flag: the name and meaning vary, and a preview can
still contact a repository. If you are unsure, decline.
Never add a flag merely to answer Yes to everything.
The second branch in Figure~\ref{fig:35-decision} is a
perfectly valid outcome, even when the command is missing.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-35/decision.tex}
\caption{An unresolved command need not end in an installation.}
\label{fig:35-decision}
\VisualDescription{Lookup leads to reviewing a supported plan.
Different arrows from that review lead to approval or to leaving
the machine unchanged.}
\end{figure}

For an authorized change, back up relevant data and configuration
first. Save a note of the manager, package and old version as
appropriate, and make sure you have time to verify normal work
afterward. Don't assume reinstalling a package restores its old
version or its old data format. Removal may leave user settings,
or remove dependencies no longer considered needed. It is not
an undo button. If verification fails after an upgrade, preserve
the old data and consult the supported recovery path rather
than trying several installers in quick succession.

\subsection{What if the wanted name is already present?}
After an authorized install, check the location before
checking the version. If the selected path still points to
an older copy you intended to keep, don't overwrite it.
Find out whether the package has a distinct command name
or a documented full path; running that exact trusted
tool on a disposable fixture is a useful comparison.
Check executable selection in the application or script
that needs the tool, not just in a new terminal. A graphical
application might use a different initial search path, and an
old shell might have remembered an earlier lookup. Opening
a fresh shell from the original environment makes a helpful
test, not a guarantee that all programs now use the new copy.

If you can't use optional packages on a shared computer, say
so at the beginning of a request for help. A recipe offering
a built-in alternative might be quite adequate for ten files,
even if it would be tiresome for ten thousand. Don't copy an
executable from another machine to avoid review: two systems
can use the same CPU but have different libraries, supported
features, or trust policies. Likewise, ``base'' does not mean
``universally the same.'' Linux distributions often package
utilities that another Unix treats as part of a distinct
base system; macOS and the BSDs have their own documented
ways of updating that system. Keep system maintenance in
its supported channel.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Installed according to whom?}
\Brian{The package list says it's installed. Why can't my shell find it?}
\Stephen{The list describes files the manager put in a location.}
\Brian{Then why doesn't the shell ask the manager where it put them?}
\Stephen{It normally checks its own search path, not a package database.}
\Brian{So a second copy could be found first?}
\Stephen{Yes. Check which command this shell would use and then its version.}
\Brian{And reinstall only if the evidence says an install is actually needed.}
\Stephen{Right. A new install doesn't settle a path mismatch.}
\DialogueConclusion{Installed is not the same as selected.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\begin{exercise}{Compare discoveries}
\label{ex:35-lookup}
Ask your \cmd|sh| what it uses for \cmd|sh| and for
\cmd|jq|. Explain the difference between a path and a
not-found status without installing anything.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|command -v sh| and \cmd|command -v jq|. Print the
status immediately after the second lookup with
\cmd|printf '%s\n' "$?"|. Any positive lookup identifies
what was found under that name; it doesn't identify who installed it.
No change needs cleanup.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Identify one supported source}
\label{ex:35-source}
Identify your operating-system family and, for Linux, the
distribution if possible. Name the supported optional
package channel from local or official documentation.
If that channel isn't available, do not install it.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \cmd|uname -s| and, for Linux, the readable
\cmd|/etc/os-release| lines above. Match the actual
distribution rather than merely ``Linux.'' For example,
Darwin on macOS does not mean a Homebrew install is present.
Write down which documentation applies; no temporary files or
package changes are required.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Compare a local inventory with lookup}
\label{ex:35-plan}
Select the local inventory example applicable to your machine
and check whether it records an installed \cmd|jq| package.
If no manager applies, check whether \cmd|man 1 jq| finds
a page and state why this is not an inventory check. Compare
your result with the shell's lookup. Do not refresh, install,
or remove anything.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
On an Arch-family distribution, for instance, inspect
\cmd|pacman -Q jq|, then \cmd|command -v jq|. If both
find something, the first identifies an installed package
and the second identifies what this shell would select;
they are not proof of each other's identity. If the package
is absent but the command exists, a copy could have come
from elsewhere. If only the manual is available, read its
synopsis and leave the pager; don't infer installation
from documentation alone. These are observations, with
no package change to undo.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Start with the command your actual shell selects, then
compare it with a local inventory or manual.
\item Use the package channel supported for your operating
system and release.
\item Review dependencies and removals; a package transaction is
not a promise of easy rollback.
\end{takeaways}

